{
 "schema_version": 1,
 "notes": "Single source of truth for the differential suite. R (reference/tidymodels-r/run_reference.R), Python (reference/fixtures/generate_splits.py) and Rust (crates/tidyrustmodels/tests/differential.rs) all read this file. Step vocabulary: impute_median_numeric, impute_mean_numeric, normalize_numeric, dummy_nominal, zv, nzv, corr_numeric (threshold 0.9), other_nominal (threshold 0.05), novel_nominal, unknown_nominal (recipes defaults). Splits: initial = 75/25 (stratified by outcome for classification), vfold5 = 5-fold (stratified by outcome for classification). Classification tasks whose outcome has more than two levels are fitted with multinom_reg (R: nnet with maxit = 10000, reltol = 1e-15; Rust: MultinomRegSpec) and use yardstick's multiclass estimator defaults. A task's optional `model` (`decision_tree`; default: linear_reg / logistic_reg / multinom_reg by outcome) and `model_args` (parsnip argument names) select the estimator in all three systems. Tree tasks keep predictors on their raw scale: trees are invariant to monotone transforms, and normalising would make exact ties at split points depend on the last bit of the baked values, which differs between R and Rust. `rand_forest` tasks are stochastic: R fits ranger with seeds 1..10 and records per-seed test metrics and predictions; Rust does the same with its own streams and the suite compares the seed means statistically (level 5), not individual forests. A task's optional `case_weights` {column, kind} names a numeric column of the dataset used as `workflows::add_case_weights()` with `hardhat::frequency_weights()` (kind `frequency`, whole numbers, zeros allowed) or `hardhat::importance_weights()` (kind `importance`); the column is never a predictor. R fixtures then carry the weighted fit, and for frequency weights the resampling metrics are weighted (tune passes frequency weights to yardstick) and `test_metrics_weighted` holds the test metrics with `case_weights =`. `multinom_reg` (nnet) takes no case weights, so no weighted multinom task exists.",
